# An episode: the starting condition, what is in mind, and the judge, in one
# file. Nothing else is needed to run it.
#
# Two intakes are on the table. What is in mind is the EVENT intake(e2, mary)
# and the PARTICIPANT person(paul) -- so a corpus whose multiplier hangs on
# the participant picks e1, and one whose multiplier hangs on the event picks
# e2. The judge wants e2. Only the numbers can get it there; no rule here or
# in the corpus changes.

fact +person(paul)
fact +person(mary)
fact +intake(e1, paul)
fact +intake(e2, mary)

# What is in mind. `unattend` first, or everything the load just brushed is
# still in the pool and the two claims below decide nothing.
rule <focus> = implies( { +intake(e2, mary), no focused }, { +focused } )
=> unattend, attend(intake(e2, mary), 9), attend(person(paul), 9)
fact +lane(<focus>, setup)
fact +lane_order(setup, 0)

# The judge. Ordinary rules, in the same machine, subject to everything the
# loop does -- which is why a missing verdict is read as failure rather than
# as nothing having gone wrong.
rule <judge-ok> = implies( { +picked(e2) }, { +succeeded, +score(1) } )
